    "aiUnitSummary": {
      "generatedAt": "2026-10-27T21:15:25.893Z",
      "sourceHash": "1z9b6g",
      "sourceManifest": [
        {
          "ordinal": "1.1",
          "title": "Intro to CLUE",
          "problemHash": "164d1ri"
        }
      ],
      "overview": "A single orientation activity that teaches the CLUE environment itself rather than any mathematics. Students meet the group model — up to four people per group, with work following a student who changes groups — then work through CLUE's tiles one at a time (text, table, coordinate grid, graph, bar graph, sketch, equation, number line, image), and finally the collaboration features: sparrows for drawing labelled connections between objects in different tiles, shared group workspaces, and the My Work and Class Work tabs for reusing their own or a classmate's published work. It is normally the first thing a CMP class does in CLUE, and its content is instructions and practice rather than a problem to solve.",
      "entries": [
        {
          "ordinal": "1.1",
          "priorKnowledge": "",
          "problemDigest": "Introduces CLUE itself. The Introduction explains groups of up to four, how to join or start one, and that a student's work moves with them. The Initial Challenge walks through each tile in turn — text, table, coordinate grid, graph, bar graph, sketch, equation, number line and image — with students practising the basic operations of each in their own workspace. The What If section covers explaining your thinking: curved and straight sparrows connecting objects across tiles, how anchoring differs between the two, plus shared workspaces and files. The closing section covers reusing work, moving between the Problems, My Work and Class Work tabs, dragging tiles between documents, and keeping a Learning Log. There is no mathematical content to carry forward; everything here is about the tool."
        }
      ]
    }
